.\" The manual for lib/cleanup-worktrees, which is what `git cleanup worktrees
.\" --help` opens: the script hands --help to man, since git passes arguments
.\" after a subcommand through untouched. Only `git cleanup --help` is git's,
.\" and is git-cleanup.1. -h prints the short form.
.\"
.\" It is found with no setup: man puts a `man` directory beside any bin on
.\" PATH into its search path, and ~/dotfiles/bin is on PATH. Installing it
.\" under ~/.local/share/man was passed over for needing an install.sh line
.\" and a MANPATH nothing else here wants.
.\"
.\" What is common to every `git cleanup` subcommand (the flags, the file the
.\" editor opens, the exit status) is in git-cleanup.1, not repeated here.
.\"
.\" The list of words is written out three times: here, in the script's
.\" words(), which is also the header of the file the editor opens and what -h
.\" prints, and nowhere else. tests/test_cleanup_worktrees.sh fails when this
.\" page lacks one the script reads, so they cannot drift apart unnoticed.
.TH GIT\-CLEANUP\-WORKTREES 1 "" "dotfiles" "dotfiles"
.SH NAME
git\-cleanup\-worktrees \- remove git worktrees that have had no activity for 14 days
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B git cleanup worktrees
[\fB\-n\fR | \fB\-y\fR] [\fB\-\-days\fR \fIN\fR]
.br
.B git cleanup worktrees
\fB\-h\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
Sorts every worktree of the repository into a group, opens the list in your git
editor, and does what the saved list says. Only worktrees that have been idle
for
.B \-\-days
days (14 unless told otherwise) start out turned on.
.PP
\fBworktree\fR and \fBwt\fR do for \fBworktrees\fR. The options, the file the
editor opens and the exit status are the same for every subcommand and are in
.BR git\-cleanup (1).
.PP
It is
.B git cleanup worktrees
and not
.BR "git worktree clean" :
.B worktree
is a built\-in command with a fixed list of subcommands, and git does not look
for an external one beneath it.
.SH THE LIST
A worktree is put in the first of these that fits it, and they are listed in
this order.
.TP
.B 1 protected
The main worktree (the first one \fBgit worktree list\fR names, including a bare
repository) and the one you are running the command from. Never cleaned.
Written as a comment with no word, so it cannot be turned on.
.TP
.B 2 locked
Locked with \fBgit worktree lock\fR, whatever else is true of it. Shown with the
reason for the lock. Written as \fB# unlock\fR.
.TP
.B 3 has work
Uncommitted changes (untracked files count), or a detached HEAD whose commits no
branch, remote or tag holds. Written as \fB# clean\fR.
.TP
.B 4 recent
Clean, but active within the last \fB\-\-days\fR days. Written as \fB# clean\fR.
.TP
.B 5 idle
Clean and quiet for \fB\-\-days\fR days. A worktree whose directory has gone is
idle too. Written as \fBclean\fR, so it is on.
.PP
Idle ones are listed oldest first, recent and has\-work ones newest first.
.SH THE WORDS
The first word of a line says what happens to the worktree it names. A line
starting with
.B #
and a space is off: take the
.B #
and the space off to turn it on, put them back or delete the line to skip it.
A line starting with
.B ##
is a note and is never read, which is why uncommenting the whole file
(\fB:%s/^# //\fR) turns every worktree on and leaves the notes and the
protected worktrees alone.
.TP
.B clean
Remove the worktree. Refused if it has uncommitted changes, or is on a detached
HEAD whose commits no branch holds.
.TP
.B force
Remove it even then, discarding those changes.
.TP
.B discard
Throw away its uncommitted changes and untracked files, then clean it. Tracked
files go back to HEAD, staged additions are dropped, untracked files and
directories are deleted, and the worktree is removed as \fBclean\fR would. It
reads like \fBunlock\fR does. Refused, with nothing discarded, if the worktree
is locked (use \fBunlock\-force\fR) or is on a detached HEAD whose commits no
branch holds (use \fBforce\fR), so a refused line loses nothing. There is no
\fBdiscard\-force\fR: it would be the same as \fBforce\fR.
.TP
.B unlock
Unlock it, then clean.
.TP
.B unlock\-force
Unlock it, then force.
.TP
.B lock
Lock it instead, so it is kept: it is listed as locked from then on, and a
locked worktree is never cleaned until unlocked. Say why in double quotes
between the word and the path:
.RS
.PP
.nf
lock "waiting on QA"  ~/ws/release  release/1.4
.fi
.PP
The reason cannot contain a double quote. Without one the lock says it was made
by this command. A different reason for a worktree that is already locked
replaces the old one.
.RE
.PP
Any other word, an unterminated reason, a reason on anything but
.BR lock ,
a worktree that is not listed, one that is protected, or the same worktree
twice is an error: the editor opens again with the faults at the top of the
file, as \fBgit rebase \-i\fR does. Quit it with an error (\fB:cq\fR) to cancel.
.SH SAFETY
.IP \(bu 2
.B clean
never throws work away. Turning on a whole group is safe, because git refuses to
remove a worktree with changes and so does this. Only
.B force
and
.B discard
written on the line do.
.IP \(bu 2
Each line is looked at again just before it is acted on. A worktree that has
moved to a riskier group while the editor was open (idle to recent, anything to
has work) is refused rather than changed on out\-of\-date information.
.IP \(bu 2
A failure or a refusal is reported and the rest carry on, with a non\-zero exit
at the end. A worktree that was unlocked to be removed and then could not be is
locked again, with its old reason.
.IP \(bu 2
After the editor closes the plan is summarised and confirmed once more.
Removal cannot be undone the way a rebase can, and saving an unedited list
applies its defaults.
.IP \(bu 2
Ignored files are not work. They are not counted, and
.B git worktree remove
deletes them without a word, so a \fB.env\fR goes with its worktree.
.IP \(bu 2
Branches are left alone. A removed worktree's work is still on its branch.
.PP
When the last worktree in a folder is removed, the folder is removed with it: an
\fBrmdir\fR on the parent straight afterwards, which refuses anything that still
holds a file. It goes one level up and no further, and is skipped when the
folder is or holds the directory you ran the command from.
.SH ACTIVITY
The newer of the HEAD commit's date and the newest entry in the worktree's own
HEAD reflog. Not the commit date alone: a worktree's HEAD starts as whatever it
was branched from, so one made today from a year\-old commit would read as a
year idle and be cleaned out from under you. The reflog is stamped when the
worktree is made and on every commit, checkout, pull and reset. Where it is gone
the modification time of the worktree's HEAD file is used.
.SH EXAMPLES
See what would happen, and change nothing:
.PP
.nf
git cleanup worktrees \-n
.fi
.PP
Clean what is idle without looking at it:
.PP
.nf
git cleanup worktrees \-y
.fi
.PP
Treat a week as idle, and review the list:
.PP
.nf
git cleanup worktrees \-\-days 7
.fi
.PP
In the editor, throw away the changes in one worktree and remove it, and keep another for good:
.PP
.nf
discard  ~/ws/parser      parser      40d  3 modified, 1 untracked
lock "release hotfix"  ~/ws/hotfix  hotfix/1.4  12d
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git\-worktree (1),
.BR git\-cleanup (1)
